  "conclusions": {
    "finding_1": "SPY-GLD 60d rolling correlation IS VIX-regime dependent (Kruskal-Wallis H=242, p<0.001). Low VIX: corr=-0.009, Medium: +0.115, High: +0.076, Crisis: -0.032",
    "finding_2": "Full-sample linear regression: beta(VIX)=0.00116, OLS t=2.58. BUT Newey-West HAC: t_NW=0.868, p=0.385 — NOT significant after serial correlation correction",
    "finding_3": "CRITICAL: Sub-period beta sign FLIPS. GFC/Recovery(2005-2014): beta NEGATIVE (higher VIX → LOWER corr). Bull/Recent(2015-2026): beta POSITIVE. The relationship is STRUCTURALLY UNSTABLE",
    "finding_4": "Quadratic spec (R²=0.024) far better than linear (R²=0.001) — correlation peaks at medium VIX, drops in both tails. Inverted-U shape",
    "finding_5": "Full-sample DM test: Regime beats Fixed 60/40 (DM=3.16, p=0.0016). BUT cross-OOS INCONSISTENT: OOS1 NS (p=0.18), OOS2 marginal (p=0.07), OOS3 REVERSED (regime WORSE, p=0.001). Classic in-sample overfitting",
    "finding_6": "Tail analysis: On worst SPY days (bottom 5%), GLD positive only 43.8% — GLD is NOT a reliable crash hedge. Fisher z-test: tail vs center correlation difference NS (p=0.107)",
    "finding_7": "EWMA dynamic correlation: mean=0.061, range [-0.81, 0.81]. Correlation with VIX only 0.051 — VIX explains almost nothing about cross-asset dependence",
    "theoretical_contribution": "Explains WHY 50/50 is immovable: (1) correlation-VIX relationship is structurally unstable (sign flips), (2) even when present, effect too small (R²<3%), (3) cross-OOS confirms no exploitable pattern",
    "practical_implication": "No actionable portfolio improvement from VIX-regime switching for SPY-GLD. The 8x confirmed 50/50 robustness is NOT because correlation is stable — it is because correlation dynamics are unpredictable",
    "limitation_1": "Only 2-asset case (SPY-GLD); higher-dimensional portfolios with TLT/commodities may show stronger regime effects",
    "limitation_2": "Monthly rebalancing; daily or weekly may differ but adds transaction costs",
    "limitation_3": "VIX threshold (25) is one choice; continuous regime approach (e.g., smooth transition) may capture nonlinearity better",
    "limitation_4": "60-day rolling window is a smoothed estimate; shorter windows capture regime shifts faster but are noisier"
  },
